02:02:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Tracking a bullet's trajectory and its interaction with other players in a fast-paced, online First-Person Shooter (FPS) is one of the most complex feats in modern software engineering. It requires a combination of physics simulations, geometry, and highly advanced networking techniques. Here is a breakdown of exactly how video games achieve this in real-time. --- ### 1. How the Bullet is Fired (Hitscan vs. Projectile) When you pull the trigger, the game engine calculates the bullet's path using one of two primary methods: * **Hitscan (Raycasting):** Imagine a laser pointer coming out of your gun barrel. The moment you fire, the game draws an invisible, infinitely fast line (a "ray") straight down your crosshairs. If that line intersects with an enemy, it’s an instant hit. There is no travel time and no bullet drop. *(Examples: Counter-Strike 2, Valorant, older Call of Duty games).* * **Projectile Physics:** The game spawns a literal, invisible 3D object at the end of your gun barrel and propels it forward. The game calculates the bullet's position frame-by-frame, applying mathematical formulas for velocity, gravity (bullet drop), and air resistance. *(Examples: Battlefield, Apex Legends, PUBG).* ### 2. How the Game Registers a Hit (Hitboxes) To figure out if a bullet hit a player, the game doesn't look at the highly detailed, animated 3D character model you see on screen. Calculating physics for clothing and hair would melt your computer. Instead, the game wraps every player in invisible, simplified 3D shapes called **Hitboxes** (usually a series of boxes, capsules, or spheres). * If a Hitscan "ray" passes through a hitbox, or a Projectile object collides with a hitbox, the game registers a hit. * Hitboxes are segmented. The hitbox for the head has a multiplier for extra damage, while the hitboxes for the legs might deal less damage. ### 3. The Online Problem: Latency (Ping) If you were playing offline, steps 1 and 2 would be enough. But online, you have **latency (ping)**. Because data takes milliseconds to travel through cables across the world, what you see on your screen is actually a few milliseconds in the past. If you shoot a moving target exactly where they appear on your screen, they have technically already moved on the server. So, how does the game make it feel fair? ### 4. The Solution: The Client-Server Dance To solve latency, FPS games use an architecture where the **Server** is the ultimate referee, and your PC/Console is the **Client**. They use a few "magic tricks" to keep everything synced: **A. Client-Side Prediction** When you fire, your Client doesn't wait for the Server's permission to show the bullet. It instantly plays the gunshot sound, muzzle flash, and recoil. This makes the game feel incredibly responsive to you, even if your internet is lagging. **B. Lag Compensation (Server Rewind)** This is the true magic of online shooters. Because of latency, your Client and the Server are slightly out of sync. Here is how the Server fixes it: 1. You shoot an enemy on your screen. Your Client sends a packet of data to the Server saying: *"I fired my gun at exactly Timestamp 100, at this specific angle."* 2. Because of your ping, the Server receives this message at Timestamp 105. 3. The Server looks at its internal memory and literally **rewinds the state of the game** back to Timestamp 100. 4. The Server asks: *"At Timestamp 100, based on where this player was aiming, did their bullet intersect with the enemy's hitbox?"* 5. If the answer is yes, the Server registers the damage, fast-forwards back to the present (Timestamp 105), and tells the enemy's Client that they took damage. *(Note: This is why you sometimes feel like you got shot after running behind a wall. On the shooter's screen, you weren't behind the wall yet, and the server honored their shot).* **C. Tick Rate** The Server processes all of this math in loops called "Ticks." A 60Hz server updates the game state 60 times a second (every 16.6 milliseconds). High-competitive games like *Valorant* use 128Hz servers (updating every 7.8 milliseconds) so the tracking of bullets and hitboxes is incredibly precise. ### Summary When you fire a gun in an online FPS, your computer instantly predicts the shot to make it feel smooth. It sends the math of your bullet (either a laser-line or a physics object) to the central server. The server rewinds time to account for your internet lag, checks if your bullet math intersected with simplified invisible shapes (hitboxes) around the enemy, calculates the damage, and updates everyone's screens—all in a fraction of a single second.
