ing boxes on a flat grid, and the engine did simple height checks. That's why you didn't have to aim up or down to hit an enemy, you just needed to be on the same X/Y plane. * **Tick Rate and Frame Rate:** In older games, the physics and game logic were directly tied to the frame rate. If the game slowed down, the physics would slow down too. This is because everything was processed on the same frame. Modern games decouple the game logic from the rendering, so the game logic runs at a set rate (ticks) regardless of the frame rate. So, in short, the *basic physics* are the same, but the *implementation* and environment are vastly different. The absence of networking and much simpler collision detection create a much more predictable experience. It's a key distinction to make.
02:02:45 | INFO | 🤖 Target: The short answer is **yes, the core physics are exactly the same, but the process was vastly simpler.** If you strip away the internet, you remove about 80% of the complex math required to make a shooter work. Old-school, single-player shooters (like the original *DOOM*, *Quake*, or *Half-Life*) laid the exact foundation that modern games still use today, just without the headaches of latency. Here is how old single-player games handled bullets compared to what we have now. ### 1. Hitscan and Projectiles: Exactly the Same The concepts of Hitscan (invisible laser lines) and Projectiles (moving physics objects) were actually invented by these old games. Take the original **DOOM (1993)**, for example: * **Hitscan:** When you fired the pistol, shotgun, or chaingun, the game instantly drew an invisible math line straight forward. If a monster was in that line, they took damage instantly. * **Projectiles:** When you fired the Rocket Launcher or Plasma Rifle, the game spawned a physical object that traveled forward frame-by-frame. You had to "lead" your shots to hit a moving Imp. The math was identical to today, just calculated with less precision to save computer memory. ### 2. Hitboxes: Much, Much Simpler (Bounding Boxes) In modern games, hitboxes are complex and segmented (head, neck, chest, arms, legs). In old single-player games, hitboxes were incredibly basic. Instead of wrapping a character in multiple shapes, old engines just put one giant, invisible rectangle around the enemy. This was called a **Bounding Box**. * Because there was only one box, it didn't matter if you shot the enemy in the toe or right between the eyes; it did the exact same amount of damage. * It wasn't until **GoldenEye 007 (1997)** on the N64 that a mainstream shooter introduced segmented hitboxes, allowing for the concept of "headshots" and location-based damage. *(Fun Fact: In older pseudo-3D games like DOOM or Wolfenstein 3D, the bounding boxes were actually 2D squares on a flat grid. This is why you didn't have to aim up or down to hit an enemy on a ledge above you; if they were in front of you on the 2D map, the game considered it a hit!)* ### 3. Zero Lag: The Ultimate Truth The biggest difference between old single-player games and modern online games is that **in a single-player game, there is no Client-Server dance.** Your computer's memory is the absolute, unquestionable truth. * There is no "ping" or latency. * There is no "Lag Compensation" or time-rewinding. * There is no "Client-Side Prediction." When you click the mouse, the game checks the math on that exact frame. If the hitscan line intersects with the enemy's bounding box on that exact millisecond, the enemy takes damage. What you see on your screen is 100% synced with the game's internal math. ### 4. Frame Rate vs. Tick Rate In modern online games, the game logic (Tick Rate) and the visuals (Frame Rate) run separately. In older single-player games, **the game logic was often tied directly to the frame rate.** If your computer struggled to render an explosion and your frame rate dropped from 30 FPS to 15 FPS, the actual physics of the game—including the speed of projectile bullets and the movement of enemies—would literally slow down in real-time. ### Summary When you played an old single-player shooter, the game still used the same Hitscan or Projectile math to track the bullet. But because it didn't have to argue with a server over the internet, and because enemies were just wrapped in single, giant invisible rectangles, the game only had to do a fraction of the calculations.
